# The weight p of the choice is a program variable, never assigned, so the bound is asked for
# every initial p >= 0.  Where p >= 2 the choice is reached with weight p outside [0, 1]: such a
# run is an error from which no bound may be proved, so against a lower bound on an expected
# runtime it is worth -oo and the bound fails at every p >= 2, although every run pays tick(1)
# before the choice.  Elsewhere ert(S, 0) = 1 and the bound 0.5 holds.
# Expected: unknown.  The error arm against the lower bound was written 0.5 - 1, negative, and
# the query was refused as not a QFL input.
nat p;
nat c;
tick(1);
if (p >= 2) {
	{
		c := 1;
	}[p]{
		c := 0;
	}
} else {
	c := 0;
}
?Ert[0] >= 0.5
